\begin{enumerate}
    \item First choose the president. There are 20 members, so there are 20 options for the president.
    \item After the president is chosen, there are 19 remaining members. So, there are 19 options for the vice-president.
    \item After the president and vice-president are chosen, there are 18 remaining members. So, there are 18 options for the treasurer.
\end{enumerate}
The total number of ways to select a president, a vice-president, and a treasurer is the product of the number of choices for each position.

Therefore, the number of ways is $20 \times 19 \times 18$.

$20 \times 19 \times 18 = 20 \times (19 \times 18) = 20 \times (19 \times (10+8)) = 20 \times (190 + 152) = 20 \times 342 = 6840$.

Alternatively, we can use permutations. We are choosing 3 people out of 20, and the order matters because each person is assigned to a specific office. So the number of ways is $P(20,3) = \frac{20!}{(20-3)!} = \frac{20!}{17!} = 20 \times 19 \times 18 = 6840$.

\boxed{6840}
\end{enumerate}
Final Answer: The final answer is $\boxed{6840}$
