Let $B = \sin^{-1}(\sin 4)$. Since the range of $\sin^{-1}x$ is $[-\frac{\pi}{2}, \frac{\pi}{2}]$, we have $B = 4$ if $4 \in [-\frac{\pi}{2}, \frac{\pi}{2}]$. Since $4 > \frac{\pi}{2} \approx 1.57$, we have $B = 4 - \pi$.
Let $C = \sin^{-1}(\sin 5)$. Since the range of $\sin^{-1}x$ is $[-\frac{\pi}{2}, \frac{\pi}{2}]$, we have $C = 5$ if $5 \in [-\frac{\pi}{2}, \frac{\pi}{2}]$. Since $5 > \frac{\pi}{2} \approx 1.57$, we have $C = 5 - \pi$.
Then
\begin{align*} \sin^{-1}(\sin 3) + \sin^{-1}(\sin 4) + \sin^{-1}(\sin 5) &= (\pi - 3) + (4 - \pi) + (5 - \pi) \\ &= \pi - 3 + 4 - \pi + 5 - \pi \\ &= (\pi - \pi - \pi) + (-3 + 4 + 5) \\ &= - \pi + 6\end{aligned}
Thus,
$\sin^{-1}(\sin 3) + \sin^{-1}(\sin 4) + \sin^{-1}(\sin 5) = 6 - \pi$.

