If $f(x)$ is even, then $f(-x) = f(x)$ for all $x$ in the domain. 
If $f(x)$ is odd, then $f(-x) = -f(x)$ for all $x$ in the domain. 

Combining these two properties, we have $f(-x) = f(x) = -f(x)$. 
Therefore, $f(x) = 0$ for all $x$ in the domain. 
This is because $f(x) = f(x) = -f(x)$ implies $f(x) = 0$. 

So, there is only one function that is both even and odd: the zero function $f(x) = 0$. 

