    \item  Now we analyze $a^2 \pmod{3}$ and $b^2 \pmod{3}$.
    If $a \equiv 1 \pmod{3}$, then $a^2 \equiv 1^2 \equiv 1 \pmod{3}$.
    If $a \equiv 2 \pmod{3}$, then $a^2 \equiv 2^2 \equiv 4 \equiv 1 \pmod{3}$.
    In either case, $a^2 \equiv 1 \pmod{3}$.
    Thus $a^2 \pmod{3} \equiv 1$.
    Similarly, $b^2 \pmod{3} \equiv 1$.
    \item  Therefore $a^2+b^2 \pmod{3} \equiv 1+1 \equiv 2 \pmod{3}$.
    \item  The remainder when $a^2+b^2$ is divided by 3 is 2.
\end{enumerate}
